## Choosing the Officiant's Microphone: Lapel or Handheld

A lapel microphone clips to the officiant's jacket or dress and stays hands free, which matters if they move toward the couple or gesture while reading. The tradeoff is a fixed position: it cannot be angled toward whoever is speaking, so a soft spoken officiant or a couple reading their own vows can sound quieter than the officiant did a moment earlier. A handheld microphone solves that by letting whoever is speaking hold it close, at the cost of one more thing to pass between hands during a page turn or the exchange of rings.

Neither option is universally correct. It depends on how much your officiant moves, whether the couple reads their own vows, and how the ceremony is staged. The [Wedding Pack](/packages/wedding/) includes a wireless handheld microphone as standard. We also stock a wireless lapel microphone as a separate add on, so tell us which one fits your ceremony when you book and we'll confirm availability and pricing rather than assume a default. See our [headset and lavalier microphone rental](/blog/headset-lavalier-microphone-rental/) guide for the full lavalier versus headset comparison.

